# From Lead Halide Perovskites to Lead-Free Metal Halide Perovskites and Perovskite Derivatives

## abstract
Despite the exciting progress on power conversion efficiencies, the commercialization of the emerging lead (Pb) halide perovskite solar cell technology still faces significant challenges, one of which is the inclusion of toxic Pb. Searching for Pb-free perovskite solar cell absorbers is currently an attractive research direction. The approaches used for and the consequences of Pb replacement are reviewed herein. Reviews on the theoretical understanding of the electronic, optical, and defect properties of Pb and Pb-free halide perovskites and perovskite derivatives are provided, as well as the experimental results available in the literature. The theoretical understanding explains well why Pb halide perovskites exhibit superior photovoltaic properties, but Pb-free perovskites and perovskite derivatives do not.

Despite the exciting progress on power conversion efficiencies, the commercialization of the emerging lead (Pb) halide perovskite solar cell technology still faces significant challenges, one of which is the inclusion of toxic Pb. Searching for Pb-free perovskite solar cell absorbers is currently an attractive research direction. The approaches used for and the consequences of Pb replacement are reviewed herein. Reviews on the theoretical understanding of the electronic, optical, and defect properties of Pb and Pb-free halide perovskites and perovskite derivatives are provided, as well as the experimental results available in the literature. The theoretical understanding explains well why Pb halide perovskites exhibit superior photovoltaic properties, but Pb-free perovskites and perovskite derivatives do not.
## 1. Introduction
Organic–inorganic lead $({\mathrm{Pb}})$ halide perovskite $(\mathsf{A P b}\mathsf{X}_{3})$ solar cells have made remarkable progress in the past several years. The record power conversion efficiency (PCE) of small cells has climbed from $3.8\%$ in 2009 to $23.3\%$ in 2018,[1–15] and the record PCE of minimodules has also been improved to $17.25\%$ ,[16] approaching that of commercial thin film photovoltaic modules. At this stage, $\mathrm{Pb}$ halide perovskite solar cells present a great potential to become the leader of the next-generation photovoltaic technologies. In particular, the relatively low levelized costs of energy (LCOE),[17,18] short energy payback time (EPBT),[19–21] and small life-cycle environmental impacts[20–23] enabled by the low-temperature solution processing and usage of ultrathin absorber layer make them a viable technology to compete with other more established photovoltaic technologies in the market.
Despite the exciting progress and extraordinary potentials, the commercialization of this emerging technology still faces serious challenges due to the inclusion of toxic Pb and instability against moisture, heat, and irradiation. In recent years, significant efforts have been paid to develop Pb-free halide perovskite and perovskite derivative absorber materials. However, so far, solar cells based on these materials show significantly inferior performances as compared to their Pb halide perovskite counterparts. Herein, we provide reviews on the fundamental understandings of the photovoltaic properties from Pb halide perovskites to $\mathrm{Pb}$ -free metal halide perovskites and perovskite derivatives as well as the experimental results reported in the literature. The results suggest that replacing $\mathrm{Pb}$ by nontoxic elements may degrade the superior photovoltaic properties seen in $\mathrm{Pb}$ halide perovskites, explaining the fact that all reported solar cells based on $\mathrm{Pb}$ -free perovskites or perovskite derivatives show performances significantly lower than Pb halide perovskite solar cells.
## 2. Overview: Approaches and Consequences of Pb Replacement
We first provide an overview of the approaches and consequences of $\mathrm{Pb}$ replacement reported in the literature, as shown in Figure 1. In general, there are two categories for Pb replacement: using either homovalent elements such as Sn and Ge or heterovalent elements such as Bi and Sb. The heterovalent replacement category can be divided into two subcategories based on the ways to maintain the charge neutrality: ion-splitting and ordered vacancy. The ion-splitting subcategory can be further divided into mixed anion compounds with the formula of $\mathsf{A B}(\mathrm{Ch},\mathrm{X})_{3}$ , where Ch represents a chalcogen element, and X represents a halogen element, and mixed cation compounds with a formula of $\mathrm{A}_{2}\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{I})\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{III})\mathrm{X}_{6}$ , which are commonly called double perovskites. The ordered vacancy subcategory can also be divided into the B(III) compounds with a formula of $\mathrm{A_{3}\mathrm{{\squareB(III)X_{9}}}}$ and the B(IV) compounds with a formula of $\mathsf{A}_{2}\boldsymbol{\bigtriangledown}$ $\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{IV})\mathrm{X}_{6}$ (the sign of $\sqsubseteq$ indicates vacancy). Figure  1 also summarizes the photovoltaic properties and the stabilities of each group of materials, which provides a clear overview of the consequences of $\mathrm{Pb}$ replacement. While the homovalent replacement by Sn or Ge leads to instability, the heterovalent Pb replacement leads to degraded electronic properties mainly due to the reduction on electronic dimensionality. As a result, all the reported Pb-free perovskite and perovskite derivative solar cells in the literature show inferior photovoltaic performances as compared to Pb halide perovskite solar cells.
## 3. Superior Photovoltaic Properties of Lead Halide Perovskites
To help understand the consequences of $\mathrm{Pb}$ replacement, it is necessary to first provide a brief review on the superior photovoltaic properties of Pb halide perovskites, $\mathsf{A P b}\mathsf{X}_{3}$ . It is now well accepted that the excellent device performance of $\mathsf{A P b X}_{3}$ solar cells is attributed to the superior photovoltaic properties of $\mathsf{A P b}\mathsf{X}_{3}$ , including extremely high optical absorption coefficients in the order of $10^{5}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , balanced electron and hole mobi­lities, long photogenerated carrier lifetimes up to $1~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , diffusion lengths up to $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , low trap densities of $10^{15}–10^{16}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , small exciton binding energies, and Urbach energies as small as $12.6~\mathrm{meV}.^{[24-32]}$ These superior photovoltaic properties are originated from the combination of high perovskite symmetry $(O_{\mathrm{h}})$ , high electronic dimensionality, the unique atomic electronic configuration of $\mathrm{Pb}$ , including the lone-pair $\mathrm{Pb}~6s$ the inactive $\mathrm{Pb~6p}$ , the strong spin–orbit coupling (SOC), the arrangement of polar organic cations, and the ionic nature of halides.
## 3.1. High Optical Absorption Coefficient
High optical absorption is very desirable for efficient and lowcost thin-film solar cells. $\mathrm{APb}\mathrm{X}_{3}$ exhibits extremely high optical absorption coefficients.[33] The record efficiency $\mathrm{Pb}$ perovskite solar cell has an absorber layer thickness of ${\approx}500~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is much thinner than the absorber layers used in the mainstream inorganic thin-film solar cell technologies with similar PCEs such as GaAs, $\mathrm{Cu(In,Ga)Se}_{2}$ (CIGS), and CdTe.[16] The mechanism of the extremely high optical absorption coefficients has been understood.[34–38] Figure 2a summarizes the mechanisms of optical absorption for the first-generation, second-generation, and perovskite-based photovoltaic materials using schematic band structures.[35,36] The optical absorption of a semiconductor is fundamentally determined by two factors: the transition matrix elements and the joint density of states (JDOS). For the first-generation absorber (silicon), the low optical absorption is caused by the forbidden transitions between band edges due to the indirect bandgap nature. The second-generation absorbers (GaAs as an example) and halide perovskites $(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ as an example, where $\mathrm{MA}=\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3};$ ) both have allowed transitions between band edges owing to their direct bandgap nature. The lower part of the conduction band (LCB) of GaAs is derived from the dispersive Ga 4s states, whereas the LCB of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ is mainly composed of Pb 6p bands, which are less dispersive than s orbitals, as shown in Figure  2b. $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ has a much higher JDOS, and therefore, stronger optical absorption than GaAs (Figure  2c,d). It is seen that the optical absorption coefficient of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ is up to 1 order of magnitude higher than that of GaAs within the visible light range. As a result, the calculated the maximum PCEs of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskites[34] are much higher than GaAs, $\mathrm{CuInSe}_{2}$ (CIS), and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{ZnSnSe}_{4}$ (CZTS) for any given thickness within $3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . For example, with a $0.3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ absorber, solar cells based on $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , GaAs, CIS, and CZTS can achieve a maximum PCE of $21\%$ , $16\%$ , $14\%$ , and $12\%$ , respectively (see Figure 2e). This high optical absorption
