## 3.2. Small Carrier Effective Masses
Small effective masses for both electrons and holes are highly desirable for producing efficient polycrystalline thin-film solar cells since smaller carrier effective masses typically lead to higher carrier mobilities, longer carrier diffusion lengths, and less nonradiative recombination. In general, band edges derived from p orbitals exhibit larger effective masses than those derived from more delocalized s orbitals. However, $\mathsf{A P b X}_{3}$ exhibits small electron effective masses though the conduction band edge is derived from Pb 6p orbital, owing to the strong SOC.[39–41] Even et  al. first highlighted the importance of SOC in Pb halide perovskites.[41] They found that the $\mathrm{Pb}$ 6p-derived conduction band minimum (CBM) exhibits a very large splitting and the calculated bandgap for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ using the local density approximation (LDA) decreased from 1.2 to $0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ after considering SOC, shown in Figure 3a. This result shows the effect of SOC on band structure, and therefore SOC cannot be disregarded in the effective mass calculation.
Indeed, it was found that the introduction of SOC led to an increased band dispersion and thus decreased effective masses of electrons and holes $m_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $m_{\mathrm{h}}^{\cdot},$ ).[37,41,42] For $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , Umari et  al. calculated $m_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $m_{\mathrm{h}}$ by various methods.[37] The standard generalized gradient approximation (GGA) functional calculated average $m_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $m_{\mathrm{h}}$ are 0.73 and $0.36~m_{0}$ $m_{0}$ is the free electron mass), respectively. In contrast, the $\mathrm{GGA}+\mathrm{SOC}$ calculations showed significantly decreased $m_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $m_{\mathrm{h}}$ of 0.17 and $0.28~m_{0}$ , respectively, which are close to the values (0.19 and $0.25~m_{0,}$ ) calculated from the more advanced many-body perturbation (GW) theory with the inclusion of SOC (Figure 3b). Therefore, the strong SOC in $\mathsf{A P b X}_{3}$ represents a unique advantage to achieve small carrier effective masses.
## 3.3. Excellent Defect Tolerance
The properties of defects including point defects, point defect complexes, surfaces, and grain boundaries (GBs) play critical roles in determining the device performance because they inevitably exist in polycrystalline absorber thin films. For solar cells, the formation of shallow-level point defects is desirable because they provide free carriers, whereas the formation of deep level defects is undesirable because these defects cause nonradiative recombination which severely limits the solar cell performance. Studies have revealed that $\mathsf{A P b X}_{3}$ exhibits unique defect tolerance nature, i.e., the device performance is less sensitive to defects formed in the absorber layer. The defect tolerance includes two parts. First, the majority of the point defects formed in $\mathsf{A P b X}_{3}$ produce only shallow levels and the defects producing deep levels are all minors. Second, the photogenerated electrons and holes may form large polarons. The charge screening prevents them from recombining at the deep-level defects.
Yin et al. explained from the fundamental level the excellent defect tolerance of $\mathsf{A P b X}_{3}$ (Figure 4a), which is attributed to the combination of high crystal symmetry, the inactive $\mathrm{Pb}6\mathrm{p}$ orbital, the Pb 6s lone-pair states, the ionic nature, and large atomic size.[36] Among them, the inactive $\mathrm{Pb~6p}$ orbital and $\mathrm{Pb}$ 6s lonepair states are the most important features and have been highly recognized. The properties of native point defects in $\mathsf{A P b}\mathsf{X}_{3}$ have been quantitatively studied by DFT calculations.[34,43–50] For $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}$ , Yin et  al. have first studied all possible intrinsic point defects using the GGA functional (without SOC).[34,43] They found that the defects with low formation energies such as I interstitial $\mathrm{(I_{i})}$ , MA-on- $\mathrm{Pb}$ antisite $\mathrm{(MA_{Pb})}$ , MA vacancy $(\mathrm{V_{MA}})$ , $\mathrm{V_{Pb}}$ , $\mathrm{MA}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaPb_{MA}}$ ${\mathrm{V}}_{\mathrm{I}},$ and $\mathrm{MA_{I}}$ have very shallow transition energy levels (Figure 4b). The defects with deep transition levels such as $\mathrm{\DeltaI_{MA}}$ , $\mathrm{I_{Pb}}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaPb_{i}}$ , and $\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{I}}$ have relatively high formation energies (Figure 4c). Therefore, the dominant defects in $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ are only shallow defects. Furthermore, $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ exhibits flexible conductivity from good p-type to intrinsic and to good n-type depending on the chemical potentials (i.e., growth conditions), as shown in Figure 4c. Yin et al. suggested that the superior defect tolerance properties of $\mathrm{Pb}$ halide perovskites are attributed to the strong $\mathrm{Pb}$ lone-pair s orbital and $\mathrm{~I~p~}$ orbital antibonding coupling and their high ionicity.[43] The superior defect tolerance properties of Pb halide perovskites were confirmed by other groups.[45,47,49]
Besides the unique defect properties, it has also been found that photogenerated carriers form large polarons on the picoor subpicosecond time scale,[51] whose screened Coulomb potentials minimize their scattering with charged defects, other charge carriers, and longitudinal optical (LO) phonons, protecting the carriers from nonradiative recombination.[52,53]
The optical phonon modes of the lead halide sublattice are mainly responsible for polaron formation. The unique defect properties and formation of large polarons explain well the super long carrier lifetime observed in solution processed polycrystalline Pb halide perovskite thin films. It was also proposed that slower orientational relaxation of surrounding dipoles adds a dielectric drag to a moving charge (Figure 4d), explaining the low carrier mobility observed in Pb halide perovskites.[54]
## 3.4. High Electronic Dimensionality
Electronic dimensionality is an important factor determining the optoelectronic properties of a semiconductor.[55] The electronic dimensionality is defined by the connectivity of the electronic orbitals that comprise the conduction band bottom and the valence band top. The electronic dimensionality is better than the conventional structural dimensionality for explaining photovoltaic properties, such as bandgaps, carrier mobilities, defect levels, and device performances, of all reported halide perovskites and other photovoltaic absorbers. It is proposed that a promising photovoltaic absorber should exhibit a high electronic dimensionality, as is the case for the current mainstream solar cell absorber materials including the Pb halide perovskites.
It is noted that for many absorbers, their optoelectronic properties can be explained by their conventional structural dimensionality, because of the strong correlation between their electronic and structural dimensionality. As the structural dimensionality reduces, the electronic dimensionality generally decreases, and the associated photovoltaic properties deteriorate. As shown in Figure  5a, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{5}$ , and $\mathrm{Cs_{4}P b I_{6}}$ consist of the same elements Cs, Pb, and I, but have different structural dimensionalities, i.e., 3D, 2D, 1D, and 0D, respectively. The calculated bandgap increases with decreasing the electronic and structural dimensionality, with 1.48, 1.90, 2.80, and $3.44\mathrm{eV}$ for the 3D, 2D, 1D, and 0D structures, respectively (Figure  5b). For the 2D structure, while the band edges are relatively dispersive along the direction parallel to the layers, they are nearly flat along the direction perpendicular to the layers, indicating that the carriers are too heavy to move across the layers. Similarly, the optical absorption of the 2D structure also exhibits strong anisotropy, with the absorption along the direction perpendicular to the layers is much lower for lower energy photons than those along the directions parallel to the layers (Figure  5c). Therefore, for 2D absorber materials, film growth with the orientation parallel to the layers are preferred for facilitating the effective carrier generation, transport, and collection. For the 1D and 0D structures, the bandgaps are too large and the band edges are too localized, making them not suitable for single-junction photovoltaic applications.
It should be noted that a 3D structure is just a prerequisite for 3D electronic dimensionality. A 3D structure does not guarantee a 3D electronic dimensionality, because the later describes, more advancedly, the connectivity of only the orbitals that contribute to the band edges. For example, both $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Pb}_{6}$ (i.e., $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3},$ and $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SrPbI}_{6}$ (an ordered double perovskite), as shown in Figure  5d, are structurally 3D. However, unlike $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Pb}_{6}$ , $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SrPbI}_{6}$ exhibits a large indirect bandgap of $3.05\ \mathrm{eV}$ and fairly localized band edges (Figure 5e), resembling 0D $\mathrm{Cs_{4}P b I_{6}}$ . The underlying reason is that although the $\mathrm{[SrI_{6}]}$ octahedra contribute to the structural dimensionality, the orbitals of the centered Sr atoms have no contribution to the band edges (also see Figure 5f) because of the high ionicity of Sr. As a result, the 3D $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SrPbI}_{6}$ is electronically 0D, with undesired optoelectronic properties for photovoltaic applications.
The above understandings suggest that the 3D structure is a prerequisite for high electronic dimensionality and a promising absorber candidate should exhibit a high electronic dimensionality. Therefore, the search for high-efficiency absorbers should focus more on 3D materials, as is the case for the current mainstream solar cell absorber materials, such as Si, GaAs, CdTe, CIGS, and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ .
## 4. Potential Lead Replacements
## 4.1. Lead Replacement by Isovalent Elements
## 4.1.1. IVA(II) Elements
Sn Halide Perovskites: $\mathrm{Sn(II)}$ is the most obvious candidate for Pb replacement because both possess lone-pair s orbitals. The electronic properties of tin halide perovskites have been intensively studied.[37,56–59] Umari et al. investigated the optoelectronic properties of $\mathbf{MASnI}_{3}$ by $\mathrm{GW+SOC}$ method, with a comparison to $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ .[37] As shown in Figure  6a, the band structure of $\mathbf{MASnI}_{3}$ exhibits a direct bandgap of $1.1\mathrm{eV}$ at the $\Gamma$ point, which is close to the experimental bandgap of ${\approx}1.3~\mathrm{\eV.}^{[60,61]}$ Compared to $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}$ , $\mathbf{MASnI}_{3}$ shows a stronger $\displaystyle\mathtt{s-p}$ antibonding coupling near the valence band maximum (VBM) (Figure  6b), due to the shallower and more active $\mathrm{Sn}5\mathrm{s}$ lone-pair states than Pb 6s lone-pair states. The VBM of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{MASnI}_{3}}$ is more dispersive and about $0.7~\mathrm{eV}$ higher than that of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ (Figure 6b). The calculated average $m_{\mathrm{h}}$ of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{MASnI}_{3}}$ is smaller than that of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ (0.13  vs $0.25~m_{0}$ , respectively). On the other hand, because of much weaker SOC, the $\mathrm{Sn}~5\mathrm{p}$ orbital is shallower and less dispersive than the Pb 6p orbitals. As a result, the CBM of $\mathbf{MASnI}_{3}$ is less dispersive and around $0.2~\mathrm{eV}$ higher in energy than that of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ (Figure 6b). Thus, the average $m_{\mathrm{e}}$ of $\mathbf{MASnI}_{3}$ is larger than that of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ (0.28 vs $0.19\ m_{0}$ respectively).
The replace of Pb by $\mathrm{\sfSn}$ shows both desirable and unwanted effects. Compared to $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , the absorption spectrum of $\mathbf{MASnI}_{3}$ shows a reduced bandgap and higher absorption coefficients in the visible light region (see the inset of Figure  6b), making it more suitable for efficient single-junction solar cell applications. However, the high-energy-lying Sn $5\mathrm{s}^{2}$ states make the $\mathrm{Sn-I}$ bonds easy to break, forming high-density Sn vacancies $(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{Sn}})$ . Defect calculations indicated that $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{Sn}}$ with transition levels even below the VBM are dominant due to low formation enthalpies,[62] particularly under Sn-poor conditions (Figure  6c,d), leading to too high-hole-density $(>10^{17}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ in $\mathrm{SnI}_{2}$ -based perovskites,[63] which is not suitable for absorber layer in solar cells. Furthermore, the high-energy-lying $\mathrm{Sn}~5\mathrm{s}^{2}$ states are not so stable, and consequently, Sn (II) can be easily oxidized to $\mathrm{sn}$ (IV). Therefore, the tin halide perovskites are extremely unstable to air and moisture.[64] In this context, to suppress the formation of $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{Sn}}$ and the $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}$ oxidation, the $\mathrm{sn}$ iodide perovskites must be synthesized under $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ -rich conditions, which can be obtained, e.g., by adding $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ -based additives.
Sn-based $\mathbf{MASnI}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{MASnI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ solar cells in the regular mesoporous structure were first reported in 2014 by Noel et al.[60] and Hao et al.,[61] achieving PCEs of $6.4\%$ and $5.7\%$ , respectively. Since then, many efforts have been devoted to improving the performance of tin-based perovskite solar cells. To overcome the Sn oxidation issue, a widely adopted strategy is adding $\mathrm{SnF}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{SnI}_{2}$ precursor solutions. Kumar et  al. first demonstrated inorganic $\mathrm{CsSnI}_{3}$ -based solar cells with an optimized PCE of $2.02\%$ with a high short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $22.7~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ when $20\%$ $\mathrm{SnF}_{2}$ was added to the precursor solution (Figure  6e).[65] They found that the $\mathrm{SnF}_{2}$ addition reduced the carrier density from ${\approx}10^{19}$ to $\approx10^{17}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and, more importantly, suggested that the reduced carrier density was due to the increase in the Sn chemical potential, and thus, an increase in the formation energy of $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{Sn}}$ (Figure 6f) and a reduction in $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{Sn}}$ concentration. The critical role of the $\mathrm{SnF}_{2}$ addition has been confirmed by later studies and reviewed recently by Gupta et al.[66]
More recently, formamidinium tin iodide $({\mathrm{FASnI}}_{3})$ ) was introduced to replace $\mathbf{MASnI}_{3}$ due to improved film morphology and electrical properties and has been gradually become the most popular choice for Sn perovskite solar cells. Lee et al. reported $\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}$ perovskite solar cells in regular mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ structure with the addition of pyrazine to achieve a PCE of $4.8\%$ .[67] Liao et  al. reported inverted planar $\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}$ solar cells using a solvent-engineering method to achieve a high PCE of $6.22\%$ .[68] Zhu et  al. realized $\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}$ solar cells with a PCE of $7\%$ via a sequential deposition method.[69] Zhao et  al. employed mixed organic-cation tin iodide $(\mathrm{FA}_{0.75}\mathrm{MA}_{0.25}\mathrm{SnI}_{3})$ absorber for the inverted planar cells and reported a PCE of $8.12\%.70]$ The PCE of $\mathrm{sn}$ perovskite solar cells is mainly limited by the instability of the absorber materials due to the easy oxidation of $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ . It was found that reducing the structural dimensionality of $\mathrm{sn}$ perovskites from 3D to 2D or mixed 3D and 2D can improve the stability, presenting an approach for PCE improvement. Liao et al. reported a high PCE of $5.94\%$ for the inverted planar cells based on low-dimensional tin halide perovskites.[71] Chen et al. applied a low-dimensional interlayer to $\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}$ solar cells and achieved a PCE of $7\%.172]$ Most recently, Shao et  al. reported highly reproducible $\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}$ perovskite solar cells with a PCE of $9\%$ by incorporating layered Sn perovskite,[73] which is so far the highest reported efficiency for $\mathrm{Sn}$ -based perovskite solar cells.
Ge Halide Perovskites: Like tin and lead, geranium (Ge) is also a group IVA element and thus a further conclusive candidate for perovskite solar cells. In early 2015, Stoumpos et al.[74] synthesized a series of $\mathsf{A G e I}_{3}$ perovskite compounds and reported their structural, electronic, and optical properties. These $\mathsf{A G e I}_{3}$ $\mathrm{(A~=~}\mathrm{Cs}$ , MA, FA, etc.) perovskites have different structural dimensionalities and bandgaps ranging from 1.63 to $2.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ strongly depending on the A cations. It is noted that the bandgaps of $\mathsf{A G e I}_{3}$ perovskites are much larger than those for their Sn analogs, and are even larger than some $\mathrm{Pb}$ analogs, which is counterintuitive because the bandgaps are expected to decrease from $\mathrm{Pb}$ to Sn to Ge analogs according to their positions in the periodic table. There are two origins for the anomalously large bandgap of $\mathsf{A G e I}_{3}$ perovskites. One is deeper orbital energy for Ge 4s states than that for $\mathrm{Sn~}5\mathrm{s}$ states, which leads to weaker hybridization with halogen p states and thus lower VBM for Ge analogs than those for $\mathrm{sn}$ analogs. The other is the structural distortion of the $\left[\mathrm{Gel}_{6}\right]$ octahedra caused by the small ionic radius $(0.73{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}})$ of $\mathrm{Ge}^{2+}$ , which cannot be stabilized at the regular $\left[\mathrm{Gel}_{6}\right]$ octahedral center, but departs from the center to form three short $\mathrm{Ge-I}$ bonds $(2.73-2.77\mathrm{~\AA})$ and three long $\mathrm{Ge{\mathrm{-}}I}$ bonds $(3.26-3.58\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ , as shown in Figure 7a,b.
Among the synthesized $\mathsf{A G e I}_{3}$ compounds, ${\mathrm{CsGeI}}_{3}$ , $\mathbf{MAGeI}_{3}$ , and $\mathrm{FAGeI}_{3}$ have relatively small bandgaps of 1.63, 2.0, and $2.35\mathrm{eV},$ respectively, as shown in Figure $7\mathrm{c.}^{[75]}$ Theoretical studies have shown that these Ge halide perovskites exhibit promising optoelectronic properties for solar cell applications.[74–81] Taking ${\mathrm{CsGeI}}_{3}$ as an example, the calculated band structure shows a direct bandgap at the Z point, as shown in Figure 7d.[75] The VBM consists of antibonding states of Ge 4s and $15\mathrm{p}$ orbitals. The CBM is derived from antibonding states of Ge 4p and $1~5\mathrm{p}$ orbitals. Both the VBM and the CBM are dispersive, indicating small $m_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $m_{\mathrm{h}}$ . Because of the $\mathtt{p-p}$ transition, ${\mathrm{CsGeI}}_{3}$ exhibits high optical absorption coefficients, comparable to those for $\mathrm{Pb}$ halide perovskites. However, theoretical calculations by Ming et al. indicated that the Ge vacancy in ${\mathrm{CsGeI}}_{3}$ is the dominant defect with shallow transition levels and rather low formation enthalpy, which yields a very high-density of holes, particularly at Ge-poor synthesis conditions.[78] The hole density in ${\mathrm{CsGeI}}_{3}$ may be slightly reduced by employing a Ge-rich/I-poor synthesis condition, which, however, leads to the formation of deep-level I vacancy that may act as recombination centers and lower the open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of ${\mathrm{CsGeI}}_{3}$ solar cells.[78]
So far, there have been only a few reports on Ge halide perov­ skite solar cells,[75,82] which is likely due to the instability of the $\mathrm{Ge}^{2+}$ toward oxidation to $\mathrm{Ge^{4+}}$ . Krishnamoorthy et  al.[75] first fabricated Ge iodide perovskite-based solar cells in 2015, using compact and mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and Spiro-OMeTAD as electron and hole transporting layers (ETL and HTL), respectively (see the energy levels in Figure  7e). The solar cells exhibited PCEs of $0.11\%$ and $0.20\%$ for ${\mathrm{CsGeI}}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{MAGeI}_{3}$ , respectively, with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $\approx5.0\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of ${\approx}100~\mathrm{mV},$ and fill factor (FF) of $\approx30\%$ , as shown in Figure 7f. Recently, Kopacic et al.[82] demon­ strated that chemical composition engineering of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{MAGeI}_{3}}$ by introducing bromide anions leads to a significant improvement in solar cell performance along with a slight enhancement of the material stability. With a substitution of $10\%$ of I with Br, PCEs up to $0.57\%$ were obtained in $\mathrm{MAGeI}_{2.7}\mathrm{Br}_{0.3}$ -based solar cells.
## 4.1.2. TM(II) and IIA(II) Elements
Divalent transition metal (TM) cations have also been considered as possible substitutes for Pb-free halide perovskites, primarily due to their rich chemistry and relatively high abundance, though the multiple oxidation states of the TM cations may lead to deep trap states as well as material instability against oxidation or reduction. Perhaps because of the small ionic radii of TM cations (e.g., 0.73, 0.78, and $0.86\ \mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ Fe(II), and Pd(II), respectively), the formation of 3D $\mathrm{ATMX}_{3}$ perovskites is sterically hindered, which typically leads to lower-dimensional layered perovskites with the general chemical formula $\mathrm{(RNH_{3})_{2}C u(I I)X_{4}}$ (e.g., $\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{CuCl}_{x}\mathrm{Br}_{4-x}$ (Figure  8a),[83] $\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{FeCl}_{4}$ ,[84] and $\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{4}[85])$ , which are isostructural to the Ruddlesden−Popper perovskites.[86–88] Among these
TM(II)-based perovskites, only some $\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ -based ones have been examined for solar cell applications.[83,89,90] Cortecchia et  al. studied the optoelectronic properties of $\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{CuCl}_{x}\mathrm{Br}_{4-x}$ hybrid perovskites as light harvesters in solar cells.[83] Figure 8b shows the calculated band structure of $\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ , as a representative of the family of $\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{CuCl}_{x}\mathrm{Br}_{4-x}$ . It is seen that because of the $+2$ oxidation state of Cu, the conduction band is derived from the unoccupied $\mathrm{Cu}\ 3\mathrm{d}$ orbitals hybridized with $\mathtt{B r/C l}\mathtt{p}$ orbitals. The isolated and fairly localized conduction band is below the upper band derived from Cu s states, explaining the common narrow bandgaps of $\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ -based perovskites as compared to those for non-TM counterparts.[91,92] The low dimensional structure as well as the localized nature of split $\mathrm{Cu}3\mathrm{d}$ orbitals lead to fairly localized band edges and thus large carrier effective masses. The optical absorption edges of these layered $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ halide perovskites are attributed to the $\mathrm{\cu\d\mathrm{-}d}$ transitions, which are much weaker than the ligand-to-metal charge transfer transitions for higher-energy photons. The XPS and PL measurements indicated that the unintentional $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ reduction introduces a high density of trap states, which act as an additional pathway for carrier recombination. The heavy carrier effective masses, the low absorption coefficient, and the high density of trap states should be the main limitations for the solar cell efficiencies of these $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ - based halide perovskites. Therefore, it is not surprising that $\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{CuCl}_{x}\mathrm{Br}_{4-x}$ -based solar cells by Cortecchia et  al. showed low PCEs of $<0.2\%$ (Figure  8c). Cui et  al. demonstrated $p$ -F$\mathrm{C_{6}H_{5}C_{2}H_{4}{\cdot}N H_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{CuBr}_{4}\mathrm{.}$ and $\mathrm{(CH_{3}(C H_{2})_{3}N H_{3})_{2}C u B r_{4}}.$ -based solar cells, yielding improved but still similarly low PCEs of $0.51\%$ and $0.63\%$ , respectively.[89] Recently, Li et  al. reported $(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{CuBr}_{4}$ -based solar cells with a low PCE of 0.2%.[90]
There have been several theoretical studies on halide perovskites based on divalent alkaline-earth metals in the IIA group of the periodic table.[93–95] Undoubtedly, these compounds have too large bandgaps for photovoltaic applications because of the too high ionicity of the alkaline-earth cations. Even by the standard DFT that generally underestimates the bandgap of a semiconductor, the calculated bandgaps for $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{MACaI}_{3}}$ , $\mathbf{MASrI}_{3}$ , and $\mathbf{MABaI}_{3}$ are as large as 2.95, 3.6, and $3.3\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Experimentally, Uribe et  al. reported the synthesis of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{MACaI}_{3}}$ and confirmed the predicted large bandgap by the observed white color of the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{MACaI}_{3}}$ sample.[95]
## 4.2. Lead Replacement by Heterovalent Elements. I. Low-Dimensional Structures
## 4.2.1. 0D $A_{2}B(I V)X_{6}$ Perovskite Variants
Tetravalent B(IV) cations have also been considered as potential $\mathrm{Pb}$ substitutes for lead halide perovskites. To accommodate the heterovalent substitution, the chemical formula becomes $\mathrm{A}_{2}\mathrm{BX}_{6}$ , which is derived from its $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ perovskite structure by removing half of B-site cations. As the B-site vacancies (denoted as $\boxed{\bigstar}$ ) and the remaining B-site cations generally adopt a rocksalt arrangement due to the large charge difference between them, the ${\bf A}_{2}{\bf B}{\bf X}_{6}$ perovskite variants are sometimes referred to as vacancy-ordered double perovskite with the $\mathrm{A}_{2}\mathrm{B}\mathrm{\sqsupset}\mathrm{X}_{6}$ formula. Because of the absence of connectivity between the $\mathrm{[BX_{6}]}$ octahedra, the ${\sf A}_{2}{\sf B}X_{6}$ perovskite variants are viewed as 0D nonperovskites.
Among the many ${\bf A}_{2}{\bf B}X_{6}$ compounds,[96–99] $\mathrm{A}_{2}\mathrm{SnI}_{6}$ $\mathrm{(A=Cs}$ MA),[100–109] ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdBr}}_{6}$ ,[110,111] and $\mathbf{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{TiBr}_{6}[112,113]$ have been investigated for photovoltaic applications. As the $\mathrm{\big[BX_{6}\big]}$ octahedra in ${\bf A}_{2}{\bf B}{\bf X}_{6}$ compounds are isolated, the optoelectronic properties of these compounds are rather different from those of 3D $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaB=Pb}$ , $\mathrm{{Sn}}$ , and Ge) halide perovskites. As shown in Figure $\mathfrak{g}_{\mathsf{a-C}}$ ,[101,110,112] the VBMs of these compounds consist of nonbonding $\textsf{X p}$ orbitals, rather than the cation lonepair s–X p antibonding states for $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaB=Pb}$ , Sn, and Ge) halide perovskites. This leads to rather large $m_{\mathrm{h}}$ for these compounds, particularly, along the $\Gamma{-}X$ direction. The CBMs are derived from the antibonding states of cation s or d and $\mathrm{~X~p~}$ orbitals. Despite the isolation of $\mathrm{[BX_{6}]}$ octahedra, the CBMs are relatively dispersive, due to the charge redistribution of the B cations.[110,114] As a result, $m_{\mathrm{e}}$ is usually smaller than the $m_{\mathrm{h}}$ . These ${\sf A}_{2}{\tt B}X_{6}$ compounds show intrinsic n-type conductivity.[102,109,113,115] Despite the general similarity, there are some differences in the optoelectronic properties of these compounds. For $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SnI}_{6}.$ , the bandgap is direct and located at the Γ point.[101] However, the transition from the VBM to the CBM is dipole-forbidden.[104] For ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdBr}}_{6}$ and ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiBr}}_{6}$ , the bandgap is indirect, with the VBM at the $\Gamma$ point and the CBM at the $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ point. The direct bandgap is located at the X point and is only about $0.03\ \mathrm{eV}$ smaller than the indirect bandgap. However, the direct bandgap transition at the X point is dipole-forbidden. For ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiBr}}_{6}$ , the isolated conduction band is more localized than that of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{6}$ , due to the more localized orbital character of Ti 3d than Pd 4d orbitals. Besides the optoelectronic properties, the defect properties of ${\sf A}_{2}{\tt B}X_{6}$ compounds also differ from those of $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaB=\mathrm{Pb}}$ , $\mathrm{{Sn}}$ , and Ge) halide perovskites. Taking $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SnI}_{6}$ as an example,[102] Figure  9d shows the calculated charge-state transition levels of the intrinsic defects. Most of the defects including the dominant I vacancy and Sn interstitial have deep levels in the bandgap, which is due to strong covalency in the $\mathrm{[SnI_{6}]}$ octahedra,[102] in contrast to the defect tolerance in its 3D counterpart $\mathrm{CsSnI}_{3}$ .[62] Similar defect intolerance was also reported for ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiBr}}_{6}$ .[112]
Figure $\mathbf{10a-c}$ shows the measured optical absorption and PL spectra of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SnI}_{6}.$ ,[105] ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdBr}}_{6}$ ,[110] and ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiBr}}_{6}$ ,[113] respectively. The measured bandgaps are around 1.5, 1.6, and $1.8~\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SnI}_{6}$ , ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdBr}}_{6}$ , and $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{TiBr}_{6}$ respectively. Interestingly, for these compounds, the PL emission peaks are just above the absorption onsets. While the PL peak of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SnI}_{6}$ is relatively broad, those for ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdBr}}_{6}$ and ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiBr}}_{6}$ are much sharper. Time-resolved PL measurements show that carrier lifetimes in ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdBr}}_{6}$ and ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiBr}}_{6}$ are as long as 79 and $24~\mathrm{ns}$ , respectively. So far, there have been only a few reports on solar cells based on ${\bf A}_{2}{\mathrm B}X_{6}$ perovskite variants.[105,106] Qiu et  al. first employed $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SnI}_{6}$ as a light absorber in solar cells, which achieved a PCE of $0.96\%$ with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=5.41~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=0.51\ \mathrm{eV},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=0.35.^{[105-107]}$ Later, Lee et  al. reported on the synthesis of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SnI}_{6-x}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ films for a range of $x$ , which exhibit bandgaps vary­ing from 1.3 to $2.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ for solar cell applications.[107] The highest PCE of $2.1\%$ was obtained for the device with a $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SnI}_{4}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ absorber layer, featuring $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=6.23\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=0.56\ \mathrm{eV},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=0.58\$ (Figure  10d). Recently, Chen et  al. reported ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiBr}}_{6}$ -based planar-heterojunction solar cells with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{60}$ as the ETL and P3HT as the HTL (Figure  10e). The champion device with the presence of the $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ interfacial layer showed an initial PCE of $3.3\%$ , with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=5.69\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.02\:\mathrm{eV},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=56.4\%$ (Figure 10f).[113] It would be interesting to see if further efficiency enhancement can be achieved for such 0D ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiBr}}_{6}$ -based solar cells in further studies.
## 4.2.2. 0D $A_{3}B(111)_{2}X_{9}$ Nonperovskites (Dimer Phases)
Trivalent B(III) cations, particularly those with lone-pair states (i.e., $\mathrm{Bi}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Sb}^{3+}$ ), have attracted significant interest as potential candidates for lead-free halide perovskites. For such heterovalent substitutions, the chemical formula becomes $\mathrm{A}_{3}\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{III})_{2}\mathrm{X}_{9}$ , which is derived from its $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ parent structure by removing 1/3 of the B-site cations. The $\mathsf{A}_{3}\mathsf{B}_{2}\mathsf{X}_{9}$ compounds have two typical polymorphs.[116] One is the so-called dimer modification that consisting of isolated face-shared $\mathrm{\small{[B_{2}X_{9}]}}$ bioctahedra, and the other is the layer modification that can be viewed as a vacancy-ordered layered perovskite. While the preferential formation of the two competing modifications depends on the combination of selection of A, B, and X ions,[116–118] more $\mathsf{A}_{3}\mathsf{B}_{2}\mathsf{X}_{9}$ compounds favor the formation of the dimer structure. Therefore, we herein discuss the dimer modifications first and will discuss the layer modifications separately in the next section.
0D $\mathsf{A}_{3}\mathsf{B}_{2}\mathsf{X}_{9}$ compounds exhibit undesired optoelectronic properties for photovoltaic applications, due to their low structural and thus electronic dimensionality. Taking $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ (Figure 11a) as an example, the calculations using hybrid Heyd– Scuseria–Ernzerhof (HSE)[119] functional with SOC indicated that $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ exhibits an indirect bandgap of $2.10\mathrm{eV}$ from the Γ point to the K point (Figure 11b).[120] The VBM consists mainly of I 5p orbitals slightly hybridized with Bi 6s orbitals, while the CBM is derived from the antibonding states of Bi 6p and $15\mathrm{p}$ orbitals. Because of the isolation of $[\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}]$ bioctahedra, both the VBM and the CBM are relatively localized, leading to large carrier effective masses.[118,121,122] The 0D structure also causes large exciton binding energies.[118,123–126] Figure  11c shows the Tauc plot of $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ crystals at $10~\mathrm{K}.$ Notably, the sharp excitonic peak at $2.56\ \mathrm{~eV}$ is well separated from the electronic bandgap at $2.86\ \mathrm{~eV},$ corresponding to an excitonic binding energy, $E_{\mathrm{b}}^{~X}=~300~\mathrm{meV.}^{[125]}$ Both the $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{b}}^{X}$ in $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ are higher than that in simple 2D layered $\operatorname{BiI}_{3}$ $(E_{\mathrm{g}}=2.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $E_{\mathrm{b}}^{\phantom{}\overline{{{X}}}}=180~\mathrm{meV},$ respectively).[127] Moreover, the defect calculations by Ghosh et  al. indicated that most of the defects in $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ that have low formation energies generate deep level states in the bandgap, which act as recombination centers.[121] Worse still, even varying chemical potentials from the stoichiometric region does not eliminate the presence of deep defects. Undoubtedly, the large bandgaps, large carrier effective masses, large exciton binding energies, and defect intolerance were also reported for other 0D $\mathsf{A}_{3}\mathsf{B}_{2}\mathsf{X}_{9}$ compounds,[128] which will ultimately limit their photovoltaic efficiencies.[121,129,130]
Park et  al.[131] first examined 0D $\mathbf{A}_{3}\mathbf{Bi}_{2}\mathbf{I}_{9}$ ( $\mathrm{\DeltaA=Cs}$ , MA) nonperovskites as light absorbers in solar cells with the regular mesoporous structure, where the $\mathbf{A}_{3}\mathbf{Bi}_{2}\mathbf{I}_{9}$ films were prepared using the typical one-step spin coating method. The solar cells based on $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ and $\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ nonperovskites achieved PCEs of $1.09\%$ and $0.12\%$ , respectively. Since then, many efforts have been devoted to improving the performance of 0D $\mathrm{A}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{X}_{9}$ -based solar cells.[132–141] Recently, Jain et  al. fabricated $\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ nonperovskite films via the vapor assisted solution process by exposing the $\operatorname{BiI}_{3}$ films to MA vapor. The obtained $\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ nonperovskite solar cells reached a record
PCE of $3.17\%$ , with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=4.02\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}~=~1.01$ eV, and $\mathrm{FF}=0.78$ .[140] For all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ , Bai et  al. fabricated high-quality films by a dissolution−recrystallization method, the solar cells based on which exhibited a record PCE of $3.20\%$ , with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=5.78~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=0.86\ \mathrm{eV},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=0.64$ (Figure 11d).[141] There have also been several reports on Sb-based 0D $\mathsf{A}_{3}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{b}_{2}\mathsf{I}_{9}$ nonperovskite solar cells.[142–145] For example, Karuppuswamy et  al. prepared large-grain $\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ crystals, by a combination of additive incorporation, antisolvent treatment, and insertion of a hydrophobic interlayer. The $\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ -based solar cells achieved a record PCE of $2.77\%$ , featuring $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=6.64\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=0.70\ \mathrm{eV},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=0.60$ .[145]
## 4.2.3. 2D $A_{3}B(/I I I)_{2}X_{9}$ Layered Perovskites (Layer Phases)
Another polymorph of $\mathsf{A}_{3}\mathsf{B}_{2}\mathsf{X}_{9}$ is a vacancy-ordered layered double perovskite, with the chemical formula being expressed as $\mathrm{A}_{3}(\mathrm{B}_{2/3}\bigsqcup_{1/3})_{3}\mathrm{X}_{9}$ or more simply $\mathrm{A}(\mathrm{B}_{2/3}\bigsqcup_{1/3}\mathrm{)X}_{3}$ . The crystal structure of the layer modification of ${\bf A}_{3}{\bf B}_{2}{\bf X}_{9}$ (Figure  12a) is derived from the hypothetical perovskite compound $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ by removing every third B layer along $\langle111\rangle$ to keep the charge balance. Based on the concept of electronic dimensionality, the layer modification has a higher structural dimensionality and probably a higher electronic dimensionality than the dimer modification, leading to the expectation that the layer modification should exhibit improved optoelectronic properties as compared to the dimer modification. Taking $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ , which has both the layer and the dimer modifications, as an example, Saparov et al.[146] contrastively studied the optoelectronic properties of both polymorphs by DFT calculations. As shown in Figure  12b, while the dimer modification of $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ has an indirect bandgap of $2.40\mathrm{eV},$ the layer modification has a nearlydirect bandgap of $2.04\ \mathrm{eV}$ at the $\Gamma$ point. Compared to the dimer modification, the layer modification has more dispersive VBM and CBM, indicating better carrier transport properties. The absorption coefficients of both the dimer and layer modifications are as high as that of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , because of the similar $\mathtt{p-p}$ transitions, but with high absorption energy onsets of 2.0 and $2.5~\mathrm{eV},$ respectively. The defect calculations showed that all point defects except for $\mathrm{Cs_{i}}$ and $\mathrm{v_{cs}}$ create deep states in the bandgap, which may act as nonradiative recombination centers that are detrimental to the solar cell performance.
Nevertheless, the layer modification is more preferred than the dimer modification for photovoltaic applications. However, the attempts for synthesizing the desired layer modifications such as $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ and $\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ by solution process generally resulted in the formation of the dimer phase. Therefore, some efforts have been devoted to stabilizing the layer modifications of $\mathrm{A}_{3}\mathrm{B}_{2}\mathrm{X}_{9}$ for solar cell applications. Saparov et al.[146] fabricated $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ layer perovskite films by coevaporation of CsI and $\mathrm{SbI}_{3}$ precursors followed by annealing. The fabricated $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ thin films showed enhanced stability under ambient air, compared to $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , an optical bandgap of $2.05\ \mathrm{eV},$ and weakly p-type character with the Fermi level at ${\approx}0.85\ \mathrm{\eV}$ above the VBM. Solar cells fabricated with $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ films yielded PCEs of $<1\%$ with low $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $0.25{\mathrm{-}}0.3{\mathrm{~eV}}.$ In a later study, Harikesh et  al. demonstrated that a smaller A-site cation such as Rb can template the formation of the desired layered Sb halide perovskites formed by solution processing (Figure  12c).[117] Solar cells based on $\mathrm{Rb}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ obtained a PCE of $0.66\%$ , with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=2.11~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=0.55\ \mathrm{eV},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=0.57$ . Similarly, the small $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ cation can also stabilize the layered structure.[147,148] The solar cells based on $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ gave a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.03\ \mathrm{eV}$ and a PCE of $0.51\%$ .[148] Recently, Jiang et  al. demon­ strated, by a joint experimental and theoretical study, that Cl-incorporation can stabilize $\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{9-x}$ in the layered perovskite phase (Figure  13d).[149] Solar cells based on the as-obtained $\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{x}\mathrm{Cl}_{9-x}$ films reached a PCE of $2.19\%$ , with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=5.04\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=0.69\ \mathrm{eV},$ , and $\mathrm{FF}=0.63$ .
## 4.3. Lead Replacement by Heterovalent Elements. II. 3D Structure
As discussed above, when replacing the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ in Pb halide perovskites by heterovalent cations such as tetravalent $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ and trivalent $\mathrm{Bi}^{3+}$ , the chemical formula $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ has to be modified, e.g., to $\mathrm{A}_{3}\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{III})_{2}\mathrm{X}_{9}$ and $\mathrm{A}_{2}\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{IV})\mathrm{X}_{6}$ , to keep the charge neutrality, which inevitably lowers the structural and thus electronic dimensionality of the resulting materials. Since a promising photovoltaic absorber candidate should exhibit high electronic dimensionality and 3D crystal structure is a prerequisite for high electronic dimensionality, it is highly desired to keep the 3D perovskite structure for the substitutes of $\mathrm{Pb}$ halide perovskites. For this, two ion-splitting approaches have been proposed for the heterovalent replacements of Pb. One is anionsplitting approach where dual anions are introduced to simultaneously keep the charge neutrality and 3D perovskite structure. The other is to replace every two $\mathrm{Pb}$ by a monovalent cation and a trivalent cation to form a double B-cation perovskite.
## 4.3.1. 3D AB(Ch,X)3 Mixed-Anion Perovskites
Sun et al. proposed the so-called anion-split approach, where dual anions (e.g., halogen and chalcogen anions) are introduced while replacing Pb by heterovalent elements to keep both the charge neutrality and the 3D perovskite structure, as illustrated in Figure 13a.[150] Taking Bi as an example, they examined a series of $\mathrm{MABiChX}_{2}$ $\mathrm{Ch}=\mathrm{S}$ , Se, Te; $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}=\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}$ , Br, Cl) compounds, among which, the iodides, especially $\mathrm{MABiSI}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{MABiSeI}}_{2}$ yield the optimal bandgaps $(1.3\mathrm{-}1.4\ \mathrm{eV})$ for solar cell absorbers according to the Shockley–Queisser theory.[151] Figure $13\mathrm{b}$ compares the band structures of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{MABiSI}}_{2}$ . For $\mathrm{\DeltaMABiSI_{2}}$ , the VBM consists of the antibonding states of Bi 6s and $\mathrm{~I~}5\mathrm{p}/\mathrm{S}~3\mathrm{p}$ orbitals, while the CBM is derived mainly form the Bi 6p states, which is similar to the case of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ . The $m_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $m_{\mathrm{h}}$ of $\mathrm{MABiSI}_{2}$ are 0.32 and $0.40~m_{0}$ , respectively, which are comparable to those for $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ (0.19 and $0.25~m_{0}$ , respectively). Compared to $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{MABiSI}_{2}$ also exhibits higher optical absorption coefficients in the visible light range. These results suggest that the 3D $\mathrm{AB}(\mathrm{Ch},\mathrm{X})_{3}$ perovskites represented by $\mathrm{MABiSI}_{2}$ could be promising photovoltaic absorber candidates if they are synthesized.
In a later study, Hong et  al.[152] systematically assessed the viability of the proposed 3D $\mathsf{A B}(\mathrm{Ch},\mathrm{X})_{3}$ perovskites for photovoltaic applications through a combination of DFT calculations and solid-state reactions. The calculations show that all the proposed $\mathsf{A B}(\mathrm{Ch},\mathrm{X})_{3}$ perovskites are thermodynamically unstable; they are apt to decompose into ternary and/or binary secondary phases or form nonperovskite phases. Solid-state synthesis efforts confirm the theoretically predicted difficulty for synthesizing the proposed $\mathrm{AB}(\mathrm{Ch},\mathrm{X})_{3}$ compounds; all attempted reactions did not form the desired perovskites under the conditions examined. Instead, they formed separate binary and ternary compounds. Their results suggest that due to the thermodynamic instability, the proposed $\mathsf{A B}(\mathrm{Ch},\mathrm{X})_{3}$ perovskites may be challenging to form under equilibrium synthetic conditions. Interestingly, the experimental synthesis of mixed sulfide and iodide perovskite, $\mathrm{MASbSI}_{2}$ , has recently been reported in the literature and the devices exhibited reasonable photovoltaic performance.[153] However, more recently, Li et  al. have shown, by a combination of experimental validation and computational analysis, that there is no evidence of the formation of the proposed $\mathrm{MASbSI}_{2}$ perovskite phase, and instead, the main products are a mixture of the binary and ternary compounds $\mathrm{(Sb}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ ).[154]
## 4.3.2. 3D $A_{2}B(I)B(I I)X_{6}$ Halide Double Perovskites
While $\mathrm{Pb}$ -free halide double perovskites were first proposed as possible photovoltaic absorber candidates in 2016,[155–157] related compounds have been known for many years as “elpasolites,”
named after the mineral ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{NaAlF}}_{6}$ . A literature survey on elpasolites shows that these compounds exhibit rich combinatorial chemistry.[158,159] Already in 1982, for example, at least 210 halide elpasolites with either cubic and/or tetrahedra structures were known.[158] Giustino et  al.[160] counted the elements of the periodic table which belong to known halide elpasolites. As shown in Figure 14, 7 elements are found as A-site cations, 8 elements can occupy the B(I) sites (including $\mathrm{NH}_{3}{}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}{}^{+})$ , 34 elements are found as B(III) cations, and 5 elements can occupy the X sites (including the cyanide CN−). Although a straightforward multiplication of these numbers yields 9520 combinations (without considering the phase stabi­lity), most of them, however, can be ruled out for photovoltaic applications by simply screening the B(I), B(III), and X ions based on their impacts on the optoelectronic properties including bandgap and carrier transport. First, among the 8  B(I) cations, the 5 alkali metal cations (including $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ ) can be directly ruled out because they are too ionic to contribute to the band edges. The halide double perovskites with alkaline metal B(I) cations have 0D electronic dimensionality and large bandgaps,[161–163] similar to the case of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{SrPbI}_{6}$ .[55] Second, for B(III) cations, the transition metal cations with multiple oxidation states and/or partially occupied d or f orbitals are not desirable for designing photovoltaic absorbers, because they may introduce deep defect states and too localized band edges. The last but not the least screening is for X anions. Most of the reported double perovskites are fluorides and chlorides,[158,159] which generally have too large bandgaps for photovoltaic applications. Bromides, iodides, and cyanides hold the potential for narrower bandgaps, which, however, are thermodynamically less stable.[164,165] Therefore, much effort has been paid to searching for halide double perovskites with bandgaps suitable for photovoltaic applications, and some progress has been made.[166]
Since 2016, many new halide double perovskites have been synthesized, including $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiX}_{6}$ $(\mathrm{X}\ =\ \mathrm{Br},$ Cl),[155–157] $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgSbCl}_{6}$ ,[167–169] $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgInCl}_{6}$ , $(\mathrm{MA})_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}$ ,[170] $(\mathrm{MA})_{2}\mathrm{KBiCl}_{6}$ ,[161] and $(\mathrm{MA})_{2}\mathrm{TlBiBr}_{6}$ .[171] Among these compounds, $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}$ is the most studied one, primarily because of its relatively small bandgap $2.05{-}2.3~\mathrm{eV},$ [155–157] Figure  15a) and superior stability against heat and moisture. Largesize single crystals,[155,172–174] nanocrystals,[175–178] and highquality films[179–182] of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ have been fabricated. The fundamental properties of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ have also been theoretically studied.[183–190] The bandgap of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ is indirect in nature, with the VBM located at the X point and the CBM located at the L point (Figure  15b).[183–185] The VBM is mainly derived from $\mathrm{Ag\4d\mathrm{-Br\5p}}$ antibonding states, whereas the CBM is mainly derived from Bi 6p–Br 4p antibonding states. The indirect bandgap is explained by the mismatch in angular momentum of the atomic orbitals of Ag and Bi that comprise the valence band top and the conduction band bottom.[185] Also, because of the orbital mismatch, the electronic dimensionality of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ is lower than 3D, which qualitatively explains the calculated larger (and anisotropic) carrier effective masses of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ ,[183] as compared to its Pb counterpart. Defect calculations showed that Bi vacancy and Ag-on-Bi antisite create deep states in the bandgap and thus should be carefully suppressed during the synthesis process for optimized optoelectronic properties.[183] Interestingly, for high-quality ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ single crystals, the carrier lifetimes were reported to as high as 668 ns by Slavney et al.[155] (Figure 15c) and even ${>}1~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ by Hoye et  al.[191] These long carrier lifetimes may be related to the indirect bandgap nature of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ . There have been a few reports on ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ -based solar cells.[179–181] Greul et  al.[179] first demonstrated the synthesis of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{Ag}\cdot$ $\mathtt{B i B r}_{6}$ films and then used the films to fabricate solar cells in the regular mesoporous structure. The optimized solar cells exhibited PCEs of ${\approx}2.5\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $3.93\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.06\ \mathrm{eV},$ and a FF of 0.63 (Figure 15d). Recently, Ning et al.[180] reported the first ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ -based solar cells using the planar structure, which exhibited an average PCE of over $1\%$ . More recently, Gao et al.[181] demonstrated inverted planar heterojunction solar cells based on ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ , showing a PCE of $2.23\%$ with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=3.19~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.01\ \mathrm{eV},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=0.69$ . While $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}$ can be synthesized either by solid-state reaction or by a solution process, its iodide analog, $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiI}_{6}$ , which has a smaller bandgap, can hardly be synthesized by the conventional methods. The difficulty for synthesizing $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiI}_{6}$ is explained by the calculated negative decomposition energy $(\Delta H_{\mathrm{d}})$ , i.e., $-47~\mathrm{meV~atom^{-1}}$ along the decomposition pathway $\mathrm{Cs_{2}A g B i I_{6}}\rightarrow1/2\mathrm{CsAg_{2}I_{3}}+1/2\mathrm{Cs_{3}B i_{2}I_{9}}$ .[183] Interestingly, it has been recently reported that $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiI}_{6}$ can be synthesized in nanocrystal form from ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ nanocrystals via an anionexchange protocol (Figure  15e,f).[175] The obtained $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiI}_{6}$ nanocrystals have an indirect bandgap of ${\approx}1.75$ eV. However, because of the intrinsic thermodynamic instability, the $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiI}_{6}$ nanocrystals decomposed into a product containing $\mathrm{Cs}_{3}\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{9}$ phase after several days of exposure to moist air.
The bandgap of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ is indirect, and its value is too large for efficient single-junction solar cell applications. Therefore, many efforts have been devoted to converting the bandgap to a direct one and/or narrowing the bandgap. For the bandgap nature, theoretical studies showed that substituting Bi(III) by $\mathrm{{In}(I I I)}$ can lead to direct-bandgap $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgInX}_{6}$ compounds, among which, $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgInCl}_{6}$ has been experimentally synthesized.[192,193] As shown in Figure  16a, $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgInCl}_{6}$ exhibits a direct bandgap at the $\Gamma$ point. The CBM consists of $\mathrm{In}~5\mathrm{s}$ , $\mathrm{Ag~5s}$ , and $\mathrm{Cl}~3\mathrm{p}$ orbitals, while the VBM is derived from the antibonding states of $\mathrm{Ag~4d}$ and Cl 3p orbitals (Figure  16b). Meng et  al. found that the transition from the VBM to the CBM is parity-forbidden, due to the centrosymmetry of double perovskite and the CBM being derived from the unoccupied In 5s orbitals.[194] The parity-forbidden transitions were confirmed by experimental observations.[195] As shown in Figure  16c, the measured optical absorption spectrum exhibited a very weak absorption edge at around $2.08\ \mathrm{\eV}$ $(595~\mathrm{nm})$ and a much stronger absorption edge at a higher energy of $3.2\mathrm{eV}$ $(384~\mathrm{nm})$ ). The measured PL spectra showed emission peaks at $2.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 16d), close to the weak absorption edge corresponding to the fundamental bandgap at the $\Gamma$ point. The large fundamental bandgap and the parity-forbidden transitions make $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgInCl}_{6}$ not suitable for photovoltaic applications.
In the past one year, there have been many reports on bandgap engineering of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ . Slavney et  al. reported that dilute Tl-alloying decreases the bandgap of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{Ag}\cdot$ ${\mathrm{BiBr}}_{6}$ to $\approx1.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure  16e),[196] which is close to the ideal bandgap value for single-junction photovoltaic applications. The significant bandgap reduction is caused by the Tl impurity-induced band edge reconstruction, which is supported by DFT results (Figure  16f).[196] Moreover, they have shown that the Tl-alloyed $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}$ also exhibit long-lived carriers. However, Tl is highly toxic and, therefore, undesirable for photovoltaic applications. Du et  al. have demonstrated bandgap engineering of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ through alloying of trivalent cations such as $\mathrm{Sb}^{3+}$ .[197] They found that Sb alloying decreases the bandgap slightly, with the smallest value of $1.86\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{Bi}_{0.625}\mathrm{Sb}_{0.375})\mathrm{Br}_{6}$ (Figure  16g).[197] The bandgap reduction is attributed to the higher energy levels of Sb 5s states than that of $\mathrm{Bi}~6s$ states, which slightly raises the VBM.[197] Recently, Li et  al. reported bandgap narrowing through a high pressure treatment.[198] They found that the narrowed bandgap is partially retainable after releasing the pressure. However, the bandgaps of high-pressure-treated $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}$ are still indirect and large, and the high-pressure treatment is not practical for device fabrication. Yang et  al. proposed, by DFT calculations, that by controlling the ordering parameter of the $\mathrm{\DeltaAg/Bi}$ arrangement, the bandgap of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{AgBiBr}}_{6}$ can vary continuously form a wide indirect bandgap of $1.93\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the experimentally synthesized ordered structure (ground-state structure) to a pseudodirect bandgap of $0.44~\mathrm{eV}$ for the fully random alloy, which however, were reported to be thermodynamic unstable in other studies.[199] Theoretical studies also showed that substituting $\mathrm{Ag(I)}$ by $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ can yield $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -based halide double perovskites with reduced bandgaps for photovoltaic applications. However, because of the high-energy-lying $\mathrm{Cu}3\mathrm{d}^{10}$ orbitals, the proposed $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -based double perovskites with 6-coordinated $\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{I})\mathrm{X}_{6}]$ octahedra are thermodynamically unstable and prone to decompose into an undesired nonperovskite product containing [Cu(I)X4] tetrahedra.[165]
It is noted that although halide double perovskites have a 3D structure, most of them have electronic dimensionalities lower than 3D, either due to the electronic isolation by highly ionic alkaline metal cations or due to the orbital mismatch between the B(I) and B(III) cations. To achieve 3D electronic dimensionality in halide double perovskites, both the B(I) and the B(III) cations should exhibit similar atomic orbital properties and comparably contribute to the band edges. For this, theoretical studies showed that double perovskites with all-lone-pair B(I) and B(III) cations (i.e., $\mathrm{B(I)=T1}$ or In and $\mathrm{\DeltaB(III)=\Bi}$ or Sb) exhibit 3D electronic dimensionality and optoelectronic properties that are similar to those for $\mathrm{\Pb}$ halide perovskites.[200–203] As shown in Figure $17\mathrm{a}$ , band structures of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TlBiBr}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Fr}_{2}\mathrm{InBiBr}_{6}$ , as examples, exhibit similar features to that of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Pb}_{6}$ (i.e., $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3})$ , particularly in the vicinity of the band edges; i) the bandgaps are located at the $\Gamma$ point and of direct transition type; ii) the VBM of ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TlBiBr}}_{6}$ $({\mathrm{Fr}}_{2}{\mathrm{InBiBr}}_{6})$ consists of Tl 6 $s~(\ln~5\mathrm{s})/\mathrm{Bi}~6s{-}\mathrm{Br}~4\mathrm{p}$ antibonding states, while the CBM is composed mainly of Bi $6\mathrm{p/Tl}$ 6p $(\mathrm{In~}5\mathrm{p})$ states; iii) both the VBM and the CBM are dispersive, indicating 3D electronic dimensionality (see Figure  17b). Particularly, the electronically 3D $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{InBiCl}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{InSbCl}_{6}$ proposed by Zhao et  al. show high optical absorption coefficients (Figure 17c) and the theoretical maximum solar cell efficiencies comparable to that of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ .[200] However, the $\mathrm{In(I)}$ -based halide double perovskites are extremely unstable against oxidation into $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ -based compounds (Figure  17d), because of the extremely highenergy-lying In $5\mathrm{s}^{2}$ states.[201] So far, $\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{TlBiBr}_{6}$ is the only synthesized example of electronically 3D halide perovskite, which exhibits a direct bandgap of ${\approx}2.0\ \mathrm{eV.}^{[171]}$ However, the acute toxicity of Tl makes the Tl-containing halide double perovskites not suitable for practical optoelectronic applications. Moreover, Xiao et  al. studied the defect properties of these electronically 3D perovskites by taking ${\mathrm{Cs}}_{2}{\mathrm{TlBiBr}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Fr}_{2}\mathrm{InBiBr}_{6}$ as examples.[203] Particularly, they found that the B(I)-on-B(III) and B(III)-on-B(I) cation antisites create deep states in the bandgap (Figure  17e), which may cause signi­ ficant recombination, detrimentally affecting the carrier lifetime and $V_{\mathrm{OC}},$ as is the case of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{ZnSn}(\mathrm{Se},\mathrm{S})_{4}$ .[204–206] To optimize the optoelectronic performance of these halide double perovskites, the formation of these detrimental deep defects should be suppressed by carefully controlling the synthesis conditions. Overall, it seems quite challenging to support highefficiency solar cells with $\mathrm{Pb}$ -free halide double perovskites.[185]
## 5. Concluding Remarks
We have reviewed the possible approaches for and consequences of $\mathrm{Pb}$ replacement. It has been shown that the superior photovoltaic properties of $\mathrm{Pb}$ halide perovskites are originated from the combination of high perovskite symmetry $(O_{\mathrm{h}})$ , high electronic dimensionality, the lone-pair $\mathrm{Pb}~6s.$ the inactive Pb 6p, the SOC, the arrangement of polar organic cations, and the ionic nature of halides. Substituting Pb by heterovalent nontoxic elements such as $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{I})$ , Sb(III), and Bi(III) and isovalent elements such as ${\mathrm{Ge}}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathrm{Sn(II)}$ can maintain the electronic features of lone-pair s and inactive $p$ orbitals. $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{I})$ is rather unstable due to the high energy position of In 5s states. Sb(III) and Bi(III) substitutions lead to perovskite alternatives with either low structural or electronic dimensionality, significantly limiting the performance of the resulting solar cells. However, these perovskite alternatives may have potentials in other applications such as photodetectors and light emitting devices. Replacing $\mathrm{Pb}$ by $\mathrm{Sn(II)}$ or ${\mathrm{Ge}}({\mathrm{II}})$ resulting in perovskites that maintain both 3D structural and electronic dimensionality. $\mathrm{Sn(II)}$ is a better choice than Ge(II) for $\mathrm{Pb}$ substitution due to its lower-cost and higher stability. Indeed, Sn perovskite solar cells have shown the best performance among all $\mathrm{Pb}$ -free halide perovskite solar cells. It should be noted that $\mathrm{Sn(II)}$ is less stable than ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{II}})$ due to the fact that $\displaystyle\mathrm{Sn}~5s$ is higher in energy than $\mathrm{Pb}~6s$ . To further improve the PCE of Sn perovskite solar cells, efforts must be paid to mitigate the instability issue. Possible strategies include using mixed $\mathrm{2D}/3\mathrm{D}$ structures to avoid $\mathrm{\sfSn}$ loss, incorporating donor elements or antioxidants to suppress oxidation of $\mathrm{Sn(II)}$ to $\mathrm{Sn(IV)}$ , etc.
Keywords
double perovskites, halide perovskites, lead-free, optoelectronic properties, solar cells
